<section class="section " id="bio">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="intro"><div data-reveal><p class="kicker">Biography</p><p class="statement" data-mt="26">Music, stories and technology as <em>one practice</em>, not three careers.</p></div><div class="prose" data-reveal><p>CR Srikanth, also known as Ravi Chandra Srikanth Cherukupalli, is a composer, recording artist, writer of literary fiction and builder of tools for musical creation. He works across all four, and treats them as one practice: different ways for the same imagination to take form.</p><p>As a composer he works between traditions. His musical foundation is in Western classical music as well as Carnatic and Hindustani music, and the influence of all three runs through a catalogue he numbers by opus: symphonies such as Pi and Hope, string quartets, nocturnes, sonatas, fugues, études, waltzes and a long series of symphonic serenades. Selected scores are published for free on IMSLP, the International Music Score Library Project, and in spring 2026 his piano works were performed at the University of Rhode Island Composers’ Concert, where he was the guest composer. He completed Berklee Online’s Music Composition for Film and TV 2 and is a registered ASCAP composer and publisher.</p><p>As a recording artist he created VS Pop™, short for Very Smooth Pop: songs that join pop songwriting and electronic production to cinematic textures and orchestral colour. VS Pop™ releases are distributed worldwide, have been played on college, community and FM radio in several countries, and have appeared on independent charts.</p><p>As an author he writes literary fiction about memory, family, identity, moral choice and the passage of time. His stories have been published by The Hamilton Stone Review and Half and One. His books, published in 2026, include the collection Tales from Bellavere and Lindenfeld, the novella The Debt of the Fifth Month, and the novels August 18th, GOTTESGABE, Kamil &amp; Hassan Drama Company and The House on the Hill Above the River. Several of them share towns and families, so they can be read as one connected world.</p><p>As an innovator he builds the tools he wants as a composer. A software architect by profession, he has spent years on algorithmic composition, counterpoint and raga engines, and now on CuTTI: CuTTI Studio, an assistant arranger that turns a brief or a score into a ready-to-work session in the DAW, and CuTTI Score, which turns printed music into editable, playable scores. Both are private and in active development, and both rest on one principle: the composer stays in charge.</p></div></div>
</div>
</section>
